# L §5.5 A set that isn't a product: "(x1, y1) and (x2, y2) give A, the cross pairs B), the set is split by the
# axes in id order: the first axis's options are grouped by the set of the other axes' combinations they give the
# result with, each group is a line ... Every line then names a product."  "The axis id in a key position reads
# the input at the current option": vx[ax] == vy[ay] is true exactly on the pairs (x1, y1) and (x2, y2).
# Lines sort by the axes' options in declaration order, axes in id order (L §5.18 Output).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2016-03-01
  start 2015-11-01
  end 2016-10-31

source s "s"

axis ax
  x1 "first"
  x2 "second"
axis ay
  y1 "first"
  y2 "second"

input vx[a in ax]: int
input vy[b in ay]: int

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2016-01-01
  vx[x1] = 1
  vx[x2] = 2
  vy[y1] = 1
  vy[y2] = 2

ask q1: vx[ax] == vy[ay]
=== expect
q1 [ax = x1, ay = y1]: true
  reading: ax = x1
  reading: ay = y1
  cut 2016-03-01
q1 [ax = x1, ay = y2]: false
  reading: ax = x1
  reading: ay = y2
  cut 2016-03-01
q1 [ax = x2, ay = y1]: false
  reading: ax = x2
  reading: ay = y1
  cut 2016-03-01
q1 [ax = x2, ay = y2]: true
  reading: ax = x2
  reading: ay = y2
  cut 2016-03-01
